{title: The Water Is Wide}
{artist: Traditional, collected by Cecil Sharp, 1906}
{key: D}
{tempo: 80}
{difficulty: intermediate}
{tags: folk, ballad}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
The [D]water is [G]wide, I [D]cannot get o'er
And [Bm]neither [G]have I [A]wings to fly
Give [D]me a [G]boat that can [D]carry [Bm]two
And [G]both shall [A]row, my love and [D]I

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
There [D]is a [G]ship and she [D]sails the sea
She's [Bm]loaded [G]deep as [A]deep can be
But [D]not so [G]deep as the [D]love I'm [Bm]in
I [G]know not [A]if I sink or [D]swim

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
I [D]leaned my [G]back against an [D]oak
Thinking [Bm]it was a [G]trusty [A]tree
But [D]first it [G]bent and [D]then it [Bm]broke
And [G]so did my [A]false love to [D]me
